# Proton Drive backup mappings
# =============================================================================
#
# Format:   <local source>  ->  <remote destination folder>
#
# SINGLE RULE: the CONTENTS of the source are placed into the remote folder.
# The remote folder may therefore carry a different name from the local one,
# which is what lets you fit an existing Drive layout.
#
#   photos  ->  /my-files/Family pictures
#   ... uploads the contents of ~/Documents/drive/photos into "Family pictures".
#   It does NOT create "/my-files/Family pictures/photos".
#
# Special keys:
#   *   catch-all, for any subfolder not listed below. The %name% token is
#       replaced with the local folder's name.
#   .   files sitting directly at the root of the source directory
#       (outside any subfolder).
#
# Check your rules without transferring anything:
#   proton-drive-backup.sh --dry-run
#
# A source that is missing or empty is simply skipped, with a log line: it does
# not fail the backup.
#
# Missing remote folders are created automatically, intermediate levels
# included.
#
# CAUTION - renaming on the Drive side: destinations are addressed by path (the
# CLI does not accept UIDs). If you rename or move a destination folder from the
# web or mobile app, its path becomes vacant. The script detects this through
# the recorded UID and STOPS that destination instead of recreating it, which
# would split your content in two. To recover: either restore the original name
# on the Drive, or update the relevant line below with the new path.
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
# =============================================================================


# --- Explicit mappings -------------------------------------------------------
# Uncomment and adapt to your own Drive layout.
#
# photos      ->  /my-files/Family pictures
# documents   ->  /my-files/Paperwork
# music       ->  /my-files/Music library
# configs     ->  /my-files/machine-configs


# --- Default rules -----------------------------------------------------------
# Every subfolder keeps its name under /my-files/drive, and loose files land at
# the root of that folder.

.  ->  /my-files/drive
*  ->  /my-files/drive/%name%
